00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:27.426
And how can you connect
businesses to their employees?

00:02:27.426 --> 00:02:29.050
We're going to run
into some challenges

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.430
when we try to build
one of these things.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.200
A lot of the systems
that we've designed

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.840
are just not ready for this.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.330
So let's look at those.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:38.170
The first one is we need to
provide an intuitive user

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:40.360
experience using
natural language.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.450
If you would have talked to a
chatbot just a few years ago,

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.880
you'd find the conversation
to be very restrictive.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.310
If you followed
the script exactly,

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:49.460
you might get good results.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.350
But if you wander at all,
it would get confused,

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:52.766
it would get lost,
and it would be

00:02:52.766 --> 00:02:54.135
a very frustrating experience.

00:02:54.135 --> 00:02:56.760
What we're going to see today is
this has changed dramatically.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.020
Dialogflow, which
we'll talk about,

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.390
is going to provide a
very good and intuitive

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:02.969
conversational
experience for us.

00:03:02.969 --> 00:03:04.510
So it'll be like
talking to a person.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:05.400
And that's our goal.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.390
This should be a
natural experience

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:12.186
that doesn't require the user
to adapt how they communicate.

00:03:12.186 --> 00:03:14.560
We're going to connect that
new experience to an existing

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:15.430
enterprise system.

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:16.390
And that's a challenge.

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:18.490
When we built our original
enterprise systems,

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:20.380
we weren't thinking
about speaking to them.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:21.796
We weren't thinking
about any kind

00:03:21.796 --> 00:03:23.200
of conversational interface.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.090
So we need to build
a whole facade on top

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:28.260
of that that facilitates this.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.000
And then the third
part is, we need

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.430
to provide a great application
developer experience.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.140
So we need an
infrastructure in place,

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.270
we need an API
program in place, that

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.910
makes it easy to create
new applications like this

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:42.290
for developers.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:43.940
So how do we build
an application?

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:46.620
What are the parts
of this solution?

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:49.250
So first thing we're going to
talk about today is Dialogflow.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.670
Dialogflow empowers developers
to build natural and rich

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.650
conversational experiences.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.902
So you can talk with
Dialogflow, it will build up

00:03:57.902 --> 00:03:59.360
a series of
conversations in there,

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.100
keep track of what
you're talking about.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.558
And what we're
going to do in here,

00:04:02.558 --> 00:04:06.940
we're going to have that send
an action over to Apigee Edge.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:10.580
The second part in here is we're
going to talk to an existing

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.057
system to get information.

00:04:12.057 --> 00:04:13.640
In the case of my
demonstration, we've

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:14.820
got a gym on the other side.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.120
And you can ask the gym
when there are classes,

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.203
you want to sign up for a
particular class, things

00:04:19.203 --> 00:04:20.180
like that.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.490
That's a RESTful service
and it's existing.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:24.210
But it doesn't speak
the right protocol.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.610
So we'll see how to map that.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:26.947
It also requires authentication.

00:04:26.947 --> 00:04:29.030
You need a username and
password to get into this.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:30.560
It uses a service
account for that.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.320
So we need to work around that.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:36.070
There's no traffic management
on this existing API.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:37.820
So if we hook up a
million of these things

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.800
and they start
talking, we may run

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.772
into some scalability problems.

00:04:42.772 --> 00:04:44.480
And finally, there's
no caching in place.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.734
So we don't have anything to
allow this to perform and to be

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:50.150
able to handle the
kind of traffic

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.190
that we may get from
having new devices.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.927
So we'll solve those problems.

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:56.760
Finally, we're going
to look at Apigee Edge.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.218
And Apigee Edge is
what we're going

00:04:58.218 --> 00:05:00.540
to use to adapt this
to the new interface.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.240
So we'll build some policies
within Apigee Edge that

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.340
allow this device to
talk to the back end

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:07.860
and allow the back end to
respond in the right protocol

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:10.770
for the device.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:11.770
So let's look at a demo.

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:13.270
And then I'll dive
in behind that

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:15.190
