00:21:01.847 --> 00:21:04.180
And then I've got to take
this response that comes back,

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:06.730
and I've got to remap
that into something that's

00:21:06.730 --> 00:21:08.710
appropriate for the
device, so it can read,

00:21:08.710 --> 00:21:11.584
it can have those contexts,
and things like that.

00:21:11.584 --> 00:21:13.750
So similar to that, that
gives us a list of classes.

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:16.850
This is how we request the
times for a specific class.

00:21:16.850 --> 00:21:20.770
So at the end of this URL
we have /classes/yoga.

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:23.680
It's going to return the
times when yoga is available.

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:25.330
If I want to
register for a class,

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:29.920
I'm going to do a post-operation
to /classes/yoga/students.

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:31.660
I'll pass in my
information and then

00:21:31.660 --> 00:21:33.490
I'll get back a
confirmation number.

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:35.073
So those are the
three different calls

00:21:35.073 --> 00:21:38.130
that we'd make to
the back end system.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:41.490
So let's look at
how to map that.

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:44.090
So the next thing to
look at is Apigee Edge

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:47.210
and how we handle these webhook
requests and responses that

00:21:47.210 --> 00:21:50.190
are coming in and coming out.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:51.690
So I'm going to go
into Apigee Edge.

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:54.000
This is where I'm going to build
my API proxy to handle this.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:55.999
And what I'm building
today, I would describe it

00:21:55.999 --> 00:21:57.425
as an experience API.

00:21:57.425 --> 00:21:58.800
A lot of times,
if you were going

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:00.436
to support a new
mobile application

00:22:00.436 --> 00:22:02.310
and you've got an existing
enterprise system,

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:04.890
you would build an
application API just for that.

00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:07.020
So this is an API
just to support

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:08.640
that mobile application.

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:10.080
And maybe it takes
all that data,

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:12.120
and it's going to shrink down
that payload a little bit

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:13.890
and make it something
that's appropriate

00:22:13.890 --> 00:22:15.471
for a mobile device.

00:22:15.471 --> 00:22:16.720
Think of this in the same way.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:18.220
But instead of
mobile, this is going

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:19.330
to be a voice application.

00:22:19.330 --> 00:22:22.230
So we're going to take
that existing response

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:26.030
and map it down to
something in here.

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:28.270
So let's look at
what that looks like.

00:22:28.270 --> 00:22:30.209
I'm going to click on
API Proxies over here,

00:22:30.209 --> 00:22:32.500
and we'll open up the proxy
that I built just for this.

00:22:36.162 --> 00:22:37.620
So we've got Booking
Agent in here.

00:22:40.308 --> 00:22:43.511
Now, if you noticed on my last
application, the URL's changed.

00:22:43.511 --> 00:22:45.760
So there's going to be
something different on the URL,

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:47.510
depending on what type
of thing I'm doing.

00:22:47.510 --> 00:22:51.860
There's /classes, /classes/yoga,
/classes/yoga/students,

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:53.060
things like that.

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:55.570
So I have a need
to change that URL.

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:58.900
So what I've done down here is
I've defined a back end target.

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:02.020
And within there, I've got a
little curly brace thing here

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:03.560
that says Action Suffix.

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:05.560
That's a variable that
I'm going to set in here.

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:09.310
I'm going to pull that out
of the webhook request that's

00:23:09.310 --> 00:23:10.360
coming in.

00:23:10.360 --> 00:23:12.860
And I'm going to map that to
the right URL for the back end.

00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:15.484
And the way I've done that is to
put that on the end of the URL

00:23:15.484 --> 00:23:20.380
here, as a variable that
will be satisfied in here.

00:23:20.380 --> 00:23:23.750
Let's click over to Develop
and see what's in there.

00:23:23.750 --> 00:23:25.840
So within Apigee
Edge, this is where

00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:27.372
we would build our API proxy.

00:23:27.372 --> 00:23:29.080
This is where I'm
going to do my mapping.

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:31.990
So a few things are
called on the request.

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:33.370
We'll see the
response come back.

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:35.069
That's what's
depicted over here.

00:23:35.069 --> 00:23:36.610
I've got a number
of policies in here

00:23:36.610 --> 00:23:39.790
that are going to manage
this request and response

00:23:39.790 --> 00:23:41.330
flow for me.

00:23:41.330 --> 00:23:43.990
And then down here,
I've got some actions.

00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:46.367
I have a preflow which
is this stuff up here.

00:23:46.367 --> 00:23:47.950
This gets called
every time, no matter

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:49.158
what's on the end of the URL.

00:23:49.158 --> 00:23:51.490
So these are things that
I always want it to do.

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:53.410
The next three are
conditional things.

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:56.740
So I've defined one of these
for every action that's sent in.

00:23:56.740 --> 00:24:00.550
So if somebody asks what classes
are available on Friday, well,

00:24:00.550 --> 00:24:01.780
we'd find which classes.

00:24:01.780 --> 00:24:03.250
That would be that one.

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:05.329
And I have one of
these for every action.

00:24:05.329 --> 00:24:07.120
And then there's a
post-flow down here that

00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:08.840
gets called unconditionally.

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:10.160
I don't have anything in there.

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:12.289
So for every time
somebody invokes this--

00:24:12.289 --> 00:24:14.830
no matter what they put on the
end or no matter what they say

00:24:14.830 --> 00:24:15.470
to it--

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:16.960
this will get invoked.

00:24:16.960 --> 00:24:20.230
And then one of these will be
invoked conditionally based

00:24:20.230 --> 00:24:22.337
on what's matched in there.

00:24:22.337 --> 00:24:24.670
So let's dig into that in
just a little bit more detail.

00:24:29.044 --> 00:24:31.210
So the first thing we want
to do when this is called

00:24:31.210 --> 00:24:33.160
is I want to verify an API key.

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:36.040
When I set up my webhook in
there, I put in an API key.

00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:37.570
It's passed as a header.

00:24:37.570 --> 00:24:40.060
That will identify which
application is calling in

00:24:40.060 --> 00:24:42.040
and which one's talking to me.

00:24:42.040 --> 00:24:44.960
I want to keep track of that so
I can collect analytics on it.

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:47.307
And I also want to make sure
it's an authorized agent.

00:24:47.307 --> 00:24:49.390
I might have different
traffic management policies

00:24:49.390 --> 00:24:51.850
in here based on what
that application is

00:24:51.850 --> 00:24:53.140
and what it's doing.

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:56.290
So by processing that API
key, I get a little bit

00:24:56.290 --> 00:24:57.284
of security in there.

00:24:57.284 --> 00:24:59.200
But I also get a lot of
rich metadata in there

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:01.960
about who the caller is.

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:03.830
I've put a spike
arrest policy in here.

00:25:03.830 --> 00:25:07.120
So if I deploy this application
and it gets really popular--

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:09.510
maybe somebody runs a Super
Bowl ad or something--

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:12.051
I don't want a million of these
things talking to my back end

00:25:12.051 --> 00:25:13.060
and crashing everything.

00:25:13.060 --> 00:25:15.268
So this spike arrest is
there just to shut that down,

00:25:15.268 --> 00:25:18.450
if too many of these
call in too quickly.

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:19.870
I'm going to remove
some headers.

00:25:19.870 --> 00:25:23.050
So Dialogflow will send me
a very specific request.

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:26.139
It's got a bunch of headers in
there that are needed in here,

00:25:26.139 --> 00:25:28.680
but the back end doesn't need
those, doesn't understand them,

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:30.471
and it's just not
appropriate to send them.

00:25:30.471 --> 00:25:33.010
So what this code
in here will do

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:34.450
is, we're going
to set the content

00:25:34.450 --> 00:25:37.736
type to Application JSON, which
is what my back end expects.

00:25:37.736 --> 00:25:40.360
And then we're going to remove
a few different headers in there

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:41.140
that we just don't need.

00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:42.765
So it'll take those
out of the requests

00:25:42.765 --> 00:25:44.750
and reform the request.

00:25:44.750 --> 00:25:46.220
The next one is get action.

00:25:46.220 --> 00:25:51.940
So this is going to grab
that action from the webhook

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:53.020
request that's sent in.

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:56.380
So the webhook request,
within the body of that,

00:25:56.380 --> 00:25:58.734
there's an action and
there's a session ID.

00:25:58.734 --> 00:26:00.900
And those are two pieces
of information that I need.

00:26:03.410 --> 00:26:05.220
We'll save the action.

00:26:05.220 --> 00:26:06.350
So this is the analytics.

00:26:06.350 --> 00:26:08.350
This is the statistics
collector in Apigee Edge.

00:26:08.350 --> 00:26:11.660
What this is going to do is keep
track of what the actions are.

00:26:11.660 --> 00:26:13.550
So I want to have a
report at some point,

00:26:13.550 --> 00:26:16.389
and I want to have different
stats on who's calling what

00:26:16.389 --> 00:26:18.180
and how popular these
different things are.

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:23.530
This next policy in here
is a key-value lookup.

00:26:23.530 --> 00:26:25.210
So this is going
to be where we're

00:26:25.210 --> 00:26:27.610
going to get the username
and password for the back end

00:26:27.610 --> 00:26:28.570
system.

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:31.120
The back end system in this
case requires a service account.

00:26:31.120 --> 00:26:32.720
It's got a username
and password.

00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:35.380
We'll pull that out of an
encrypted key-value map.

00:26:35.380 --> 00:26:37.460
And then this policy
will set the password,

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:39.790
so we can get authenticated
with the back end system.

00:26:39.790 --> 00:26:41.800
So that's going to
happen with every call.

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:43.450
Let's look at the
first one in here

00:26:43.450 --> 00:26:47.220
where we say Find Which Classes.

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:48.970
So in this case, they've
specified a date.

00:26:48.970 --> 00:26:52.780
Which classes are
available on Friday?

00:26:52.780 --> 00:26:54.722
We're going to grab
the date out of there

00:26:54.722 --> 00:26:56.430
and we're going to
get the original date.

00:26:56.430 --> 00:27:00.230
So date is the calendar
date and then date original

00:27:00.230 --> 00:27:01.420
is the spoken date.

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:04.080
The reason I want to grab
this is when I put together

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:05.790
the response, I
want to tell them

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:09.902
which classes are available
on Friday and not 5, 15, 2018.

00:27:09.902 --> 00:27:11.610
I want to speak back
to them the same way

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:13.930
that they ask the question.

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:15.840
So that's the
request side of this.

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:18.780
Down on the response side, I'm
going to get that response back

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:19.630
from--

00:27:19.630 --> 00:27:22.800
let's look over here for
what that one looks like.

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:25.050
I'm going to get a response
back that looks like this.

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:27.350
So an array of classes in there.

00:27:27.350 --> 00:27:30.140
And I need to translate that
into an appropriate webhook

00:27:30.140 --> 00:27:32.660
response for Dialogflow.

00:27:32.660 --> 00:27:34.410
So that's what's going
to happen down here

00:27:34.410 --> 00:27:35.700
in this JavaScript policy.

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:36.690
So classes ToString.

00:27:39.186 --> 00:27:40.560
So what this does
is this is just

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:42.393
going to iterate over
that array and turn it

00:27:42.393 --> 00:27:44.970
into a string that looks
more like a spoken sentence.

00:27:44.970 --> 00:27:47.590
And we'll set a variable in
there called Class String.

00:27:47.590 --> 00:27:50.370
So if there's no
classes, set it to that.

00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:53.276
Otherwise, just make it a
comma-separated sentence

00:27:53.276 --> 00:27:54.150
and then return that.

00:27:56.705 --> 00:27:59.080
And then finally we're going
to set the response in here.

00:27:59.080 --> 00:28:01.110
So this is the kind of
response that we have

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:03.160
to send back to Dialogflow.

00:28:03.160 --> 00:28:05.550
And within here, we're going
to have fulfillment text--

00:28:05.550 --> 00:28:07.230
that's what it's going to speak.

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:09.510
And these curly
braces in here, that's

00:28:09.510 --> 00:28:11.080
the evaluation of a variable.

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:16.215
So yoga, kickboxing, et cetera
are available on Thursday.

00:28:16.215 --> 00:28:18.780
That's what that's going
to say when it goes back.

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:20.670
And then we need to set
that output context.

00:28:20.670 --> 00:28:23.620
We need to put Dialogflow
into a particular state.

00:28:23.620 --> 00:28:24.900
So that's what this does.

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:29.640
The way we do that is we send
back the session ID context.

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:32.250
And then this text
right here is whatever

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:34.410
that context should be.

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:37.435
And a time out for that, it's
going to last for five minutes.

00:28:37.435 --> 00:28:39.810
If somebody stops talking to
the device for five minutes,

00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:40.697
it'll reset itself.

00:28:40.697 --> 00:28:42.780
And then we're going to
have a bunch of parameters

00:28:42.780 --> 00:28:45.279
that we're going to pass back--
the date, the original date,

00:28:45.279 --> 00:28:49.240
the list of classes,
things like that.

00:28:49.240 --> 00:28:50.710
So that's this particular flow.

00:28:50.710 --> 00:28:52.650
If we go to the next
one, we're going

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:55.100
to look at what happens
as that follow-up.

00:28:55.100 --> 00:28:56.610
So they may ask--

00:28:56.610 --> 00:28:59.010
they know which classes
are available on Friday,

00:28:59.010 --> 00:29:02.740
but now they want to know when
is a specific class available.

00:29:02.740 --> 00:29:05.590
So, when is yoga available?

00:29:05.590 --> 00:29:08.226
So we're going to
grab out of here--

00:29:08.226 --> 00:29:09.600
we're going to
parse that action.

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:11.034
We'll get that in the preflow.

00:29:11.034 --> 00:29:13.200
And then we're also going
to grab the class name out

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:14.800
of here-- when is
yoga available?

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:17.610
And then the second one here is
going to grab the date and then

00:29:17.610 --> 00:29:18.510
the original date.

00:29:18.510 --> 00:29:19.986
And we'll store
those as variables.

00:29:19.986 --> 00:29:22.110
And then we're going to
change the requests around.

00:29:22.110 --> 00:29:24.235
I didn't talk about this
too much on the first one.

00:29:24.235 --> 00:29:27.990
But I'm always getting a
post in here, an HTTP post.

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:29.580
And my back end
system is RESTful.

00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:31.200
It expects a GET in there.

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:33.240
So I'm going to change
the verb to a GET.

00:29:33.240 --> 00:29:37.980
I'm going to rebuild the URL by
setting a value for this Action

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:38.490
Suffix.

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:40.382
So that was on the
end of my target URL.

00:29:40.382 --> 00:29:42.090
I'm going to put that
in there and that's

00:29:42.090 --> 00:29:44.950
going to call the back end.

00:29:44.950 --> 00:29:48.770
And then on this one, I'm going
to get back a list of times.

00:29:48.770 --> 00:29:52.630
So I've got JavaScript in here
to map that array of times

00:29:52.630 --> 00:29:53.380
into a sentence.

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:55.460
So it's going to
come back like this.

00:29:55.460 --> 00:29:58.060
And then we've got to create
a string that looks like that.

00:29:58.060 --> 00:29:59.893
And that's what this
code is doing in here--

00:29:59.893 --> 00:30:01.480
we're building up a time string.

00:30:01.480 --> 00:30:05.350
And then that's going to
be returned down here.

00:30:05.350 --> 00:30:09.485
So down here we'll see that
it gets returned as a string.

00:30:09.485 --> 00:30:11.840
So that's going to
be put into there.

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:15.470
Yoga is available
Tuesday at 3:00 PM.

00:30:15.470 --> 00:30:18.980
That's what that will look like
when it's completely filled out

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:20.507
and sent back to the agent.

00:30:20.507 --> 00:30:22.340
And then we're going
to set a context again.

00:30:22.340 --> 00:30:25.985
This time the context is
Asked Find Class Times.

00:30:25.985 --> 00:30:27.860
And then it's going to
last for five minutes.

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:28.970
And we're going
to send back which

00:30:28.970 --> 00:30:31.520
class they were talking about,
so we can remember that--

00:30:31.520 --> 00:30:34.250
a date and a date original.

00:30:34.250 --> 00:30:37.110
Now, third step in here, they
want to register for a class.

00:30:37.110 --> 00:30:38.960
So what does that
look like when they

00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:41.060
go to register for a class?

00:30:41.060 --> 00:30:43.980
First thing is, we need
to know which class.

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:46.220
So that's going to come
across in the context.

00:30:46.220 --> 00:30:49.610
It'll be in the parameters
under that Action section.

00:30:49.610 --> 00:30:52.750
So we're going to grab the
class name out of that body.

00:30:52.750 --> 00:30:56.637
We're going to get the date and
we're going to get the time.

00:30:56.637 --> 00:30:58.970
And then we're going to change
the request around again.

00:30:58.970 --> 00:31:01.370
In this case, it
is a post, so we're

00:31:01.370 --> 00:31:02.730
going to leave that alone.

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:05.063
But we've got to set the
payload to something different.

00:31:05.063 --> 00:31:06.770
We're going to pass
in the information

00:31:06.770 --> 00:31:07.815
about the user in there.

00:31:07.815 --> 00:31:09.440
And that's going to
be used to register

00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:12.380
for the class at
a particular time.

00:31:12.380 --> 00:31:13.892
And then on the
response side, we're

00:31:13.892 --> 00:31:15.350
going to get a
confirmation number.

00:31:15.350 --> 00:31:17.740
The back end system will
return a confirmation number

00:31:17.740 --> 00:31:19.270
for that registration.

00:31:19.270 --> 00:31:20.830
We want to return that.

00:31:20.830 --> 00:31:23.080
And then we're going to build
up the response in here,

00:31:23.080 --> 00:31:24.520
like we did in the other flows.

00:31:24.520 --> 00:31:29.630
So down here, you're signed
up for yoga, Friday at 3:00

00:31:29.630 --> 00:31:32.320
PM or whatever they asked for.

00:31:32.320 --> 00:31:34.739
And we're going to set a
context in here of Class Booked.

00:31:34.739 --> 00:31:37.030
And then we're going to return
that confirmation number

00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:38.360
in there.

00:31:38.360 --> 00:31:40.880
So that's our three
conditional flows in here.

00:31:40.880 --> 00:31:44.300
The way that I set this up
so that these would be called

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:46.580
